<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Finding Complementary Angles and Trigonometric Ratios</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>
Angles \({@letter_1@}\) and \({@letter_2@}\) are complementary. If \( {@letter_1@}\) = {@fraction@}\({@letter_2@}\) ,
</p>

<ol type="a">
    <li>
        <p>Find the values of the angles:</p>
        <ol type="i">
            <li>\({@letter_1@}=\)[[input:ans1]]\(^\circ\) [[validation:ans1]]</li>
            <li>\({@letter_2@}=\)[[input:ans2]]\(^\circ\)[[validation:ans2]]</li>
           <p>[[feedback:prt1]]<p>
        </ol>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
        Using a calculator, find the following correct to four decimal places:
        </p>
        <ol type="i">
            <li>\(\sin\, {@letter_1@}=\)[[input:ans3]][[validation:ans3]]</li>
            <li>\(\cos\,{@letter_2@}=\)[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]]</li>
           <p>[[feedback:prt2]]<p>
        </ol>
    </li>
</ol>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>When we say {@letter_1@} and {@letter_2@} are complementary, it implies that \({@letter_1@}+{@letter_2@}=90^\circ\).<br>
Also, we have \({@letter_1@}={@fraction@}{@letter_2@}\).</p>

<p>First, write \({@letter_2@}\) in terms of \({@letter_1@}\), that is,<br>
\({@eq_1@}\)<br>
\({@eq_2@}\)</p>

<p>Since \({@letter_1@}+{@letter_2@}=90^\circ\), substitute \({@eq_2@}\):<br>
\({@frac_2*letter_1@}+{@letter_1@}=90\)<br>
\({@eq_5@}\)<br>
\(\quad {@letter_1@}=\frac{90}{{@coeficient@}}\)<br>
\(\qquad ={@Ta_1@}^\circ\)</p>

<p>Therefore,<br>
<ol type='i'>
<li>\(\angle \,{@letter_1@}={@Ta_1@}^\circ\)</li>
<li>
<p>\(\angle\,{@letter_2@}\)</p>
<p>
\(\begin{align}
\angle \,{@letter_2@}&=90^\circ-{@Ta_1@}^\circ\\
&={@Ta_2@}^\circ
\end{align}\)
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>Use your calculator to find both \(\sin\,{@Ta_1@}^\circ\) and \(\cos\,{@Ta_2@}^\circ\), and write the answers correct to 4 significant figures.<br>
Therefore,
<ol type='i'>
<li>\(\sin\,{@Ta_1@}^\circ={@an_1@}\)</li>
<li>\(\cos\,{@Ta_2@}^\circ={@an_2@}\)</li>
</ol>
</p>
<p><strong>Notice:</strong> Because the angles are complementary, \(\sin\,{@Ta_1@}^\circ\) is exactly equal to \(\cos\,{@Ta_2@}^\circ\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
